description:
In TypeScript, you declare variables with `let` and `const`. Const variables are immutable, which means they cannot be reassigned. In TypeScript, it is a common trope to make any variable that can be const const. let allows you to make mutable variables. To declare a variable, you use:
```
let VAR_NAMEL: type = value;
// or
let VAR_NAME: type;
// or
const VAR_NAME: type = value;
```
Const variables must be initilized.
---
## Assignment:
Somebody at MeTube that came from JS used wrong datatypes and assigned to a `const` variable. Fix it!

